---
// /build-on-odal — how someone other than us builds on the software.
//
// Every route described here exists in the code today: the Apache-2.0 core
// library, signed product-group plugins and the Asset Administration Shell
// export. The page says plainly that nobody has built on it yet, because an
// ecosystem page is the easiest place on a site to imply one that does not
// exist. Technical detail lives on the docs site; this page links to it rather
// than repeating it.
//
// Signed rule bundles are deliberately NOT a way in. The node verifies and
// hot-swaps them, but a bundle carries only a signed version today: the rules
// that run are compiled in and no bundle reaches them (dpp-engine
// `infra/ruleset.rs`, "What a swap changes today"). Add it back when a bundle
// changes how a passport is checked.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import ExternalArrow from "../components/ExternalArrow.astro";

const title = "Build on Odal | Odal Node";
const description =
  "Three ways to build on Odal Node: embed the open library, write product-group rules, and connect industrial systems. A plugin is trusted by a signature its node's operator accepts, not by us.";

const ways = [
  {
    title: "Embed the open library",
    body: "The regulatory core is a set of Apache-2.0 libraries anyone can use: the passport model, signing, credentials, GS1 Digital Link, the industrial export and the regulatory rules.",
    href: "https://docs.odal-node.io/core/overview",
    cta: "What the core does",
    icon: '<path d="M4 7.5 12 3l8 4.5v9L12 21l-8-4.5v-9Z"/><path d="M4 7.5 12 12l8-4.5M12 12v9"/>',
  },
  {
    title: "Write product-group rules",
    body: "A product group's checks can ship as a plugin that runs in an isolated sandbox. A node refuses a plugin that is not signed by a key its operator trusts, unless the operator deliberately switches that check off for development. A new version can be installed on a running node without a restart.",
    href: "https://docs.odal-node.io/core/sectors",
    cta: "Extending it with plugins",
    icon: '<rect x="4" y="4" width="16" height="16" rx="2.5"/><path d="M9 9h6v6H9z"/>',
  },
  {
    title: "Connect industrial systems",
    body: "A node can serve a published passport in the Asset Administration Shell format that Industry 4.0 systems read. The structure follows the standard, but the field meanings use Odal's own identifiers for now, so a receiving system still has to map them.",
    href: "https://docs.odal-node.io/core/standards",
    cta: "The standards it uses",
    icon: '<rect x="3" y="4" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/><rect x="14" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/><path d="M10 7.5h4a2 2 0 0 1 2 2V13"/>',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section h1 eyebrow="Build on Odal" title="Other people's compliance work, on nodes we do not run." wide>
    <p>
      Odal Node is built so that product-group rules and integrations written
      by someone else can run on it, on infrastructure we never touch. A plugin
      is trusted because of a signature the node's operator chooses to accept,
      not because of us.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Nobody has built on it yet.</strong> This page describes what is
      possible today, not an ecosystem that already exists.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Three ways in" title="What you can build today." tone="muted" wide>
    <ul class="not-prose m-0 grid list-none gap-5 p-0 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {ways.map((w) => (
        <li class="card m-0 flex flex-col p-6">
          <span class="flex h-11 w-11 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-primary-50 text-primary-700 ring-1 ring-primary-100">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" class="h-6 w-6" set:html={w.icon} />
          </span>
          <h3 class="m-0 mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900">{w.title}</h3>
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-600">{w.body}</p>
          <p class="m-0 mt-auto pt-4 text-sm">
            <a href={w.href} rel="noopener" class="font-medium">{w.cta}<ExternalArrow class="ml-1" /></a>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="The licence line" title="What is free, and where the line is." wide>
    <p>
      <strong>The core library is Apache-2.0.</strong> Use it, adapt it and
      build on it freely.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The node is source-available under BSL-1.1.</strong> Running it
      for your own organisation's passports is free. Offering it to others as a
      hosted service, or shipping it inside a competing product, needs a
      commercial licence. Each release converts to Apache-2.0 four years after
      it is published.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://docs.odal-node.io/getting-started/licensing" rel="noopener">The licensing page</a>{" "}
      has the detail, and the licence texts in each repository are the ones
      that bind.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Why it matters" title="A platform you can leave is one you can build on." tone="tinted" wide>
    <p>
      Every node belongs to its operator, and every passport verifies without
      us. There is no central service here for a builder to depend on, or to be
      captured by, which is the point.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/odal-node" rel="noopener">The code is public on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>
